Internal os_log codec tests (op-201)

Public API (op-207): oslog_api_test registers objects, oracle and canary.
It links the installed libc and reads the real logd store in a Mach guest.
The supplied probe sources under oracle_inputs are unchanged; compiler flags
permit their original warnings. The two approved native exceptions are the
observed ASLR pointer value and A03's writable FreeBSD strerror buffer.
Separate deterministic public-entry replays still match all 74 oracle rows.
api_checks.zig replaces the internal send only for host builds. Host parser/
store iteration is a component harness; it does not prove Mach transport,
daemon startup, dispatch queues or BSD-output routing.

The public header derives from XNU's APSL header, not the SDK. Format strings
live in the readonly ELF .os_log section. An opaque local token replaces the
Mach-O dso header; log.h does not redeclare trace.h's __dso_handle. Emit entries
capture the caller PC and serialize public data only, using libc's existing
Mach send and shared syslog mutex. Public pointer reads use kernel copyin via
an owned pipe, with no process signal handler. Log objects are immortal and
interned by subsystem/category. Their prefix also supports libdispatch's
generic retain/release; libc delegates non-log objects to that runtime.

Record keys below org.rmxos.logd are type (raw u8 in decimal), subsystem,
category, formatString, threadID, machTimestamp (uptime ns), senderImagePath,
senderImageBuildID (GNU ELF build-ID hex, empty if absent), senderImagePC
(offset from lowest PT_LOAD address), and traceID (opaque decimal u64).
Legacy ASL Level is notice/info/error/critical for default/info/error/fault.
Empty values use ASL's existing octal-NUL escape, preserving empty strings
instead of null-valued keys. No store-format or logd change is required.

%S is refused without sending. Precision-capped strings follow XNU and omit
their NUL; a budget-truncated string retains its terminator and marker. The
precision-zero boundary has a wire regression. The fault budget remains the
single measured profile described below. The canary seed is host-random test
data; the case PID and a fresh random nonce make each invocation distinct.
Store/text scans stream without a file-size cap. Case reads page back to a
saved message-ID boundary, excluding historical records with a reused PID.
An ordered end marker proves the disabled/refused calls produced no record.
The guest scans
store bytes, text and kernel console history; the host validator also scans
the entire captured serial for the private prefix. Its public console marker
is an explicit positive control, since logd has no console rule.

oracle.json contains the eventMessage, formatString and messageType fields of
all 34 op963 and 40 op970 rows from the staged macOS 27 oracle. The source
manifest SHA256 is c14e12c938d7b89dbbf86e01614d5da0a96c9b819d62d863dbfe77570a215d84.
Rmx.Op201 in rmx-implementer regenerates the compiler inputs from those rows.
C fixtures contain Clang builtin call sites and storage getters only. Zig owns
the input selection, bounds-checked capture reader and assertions.

oracle compares every eventMessage byte. The main executable, a linked DSO and
dlopen/RTLD_NODELETE DSO are real native images. For the op963 pointer row it
replays the recorded numeric address, since ASLR addresses differ between runs.
For op970 A03, FreeBSD strerror() returns writable storage and is asserted
private; the byte-exact replay instead uses libc's readonly sys_errlist entry
with identical text. This does not reproduce the native macOS strerror call.
No writable-pointer exemption is added to the codec.

wire asserts the serialized range bytes, scalar formatting, precision, literal
percent and redaction. malformed exercises short inputs, invalid ranges and
formats (including %n), a rejected pointer, and 20,000 random input pairs. The
host sanitizer driver instruments the C codec with Clang ASan and UBSan; its
intentional compiler-input faults verify both instruments are active.

The codec is internal to libc, with hidden symbols and an uninstalled header.
Pointer capture is delegated to a checked reader; rendering has no pointers or
varargs to read. The normal flattened budget is 1024 bytes. The fault profile
uses 1998 bytes to reproduce 1989 string bytes plus header/range/NUL overhead;
that fault budget rests on one macOS 27 call-site measurement, not a general
fault-size contract. Arguments are charged in order, without reserving later
items. No 32-string kernel restriction is applied to user space.

Enablement and record metadata are outside this codec. Disabled/debug and the
crashing Objective-C %@ call in the probes have no stored oracle row to replay.
Object/data/errno/mask descriptors are unsupported; malformed/unsupported inputs
return an error. The codec exports no public API or installed header; op-207
adds the separate public wrapper described above. logd remains unchanged.
